meta {
  name: Me
  type: http
  seq: 4
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/me
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

tests {
  test("answers with the caller's account", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
    expect(res.body.account.email).to.equal(bru.getEnvVar("email"));
  });

  test("names the role the dashboard branches on", function () {
    expect(res.body.account.role).to.be.oneOf(["creator", "admin"]);
  });

  test("carries an id and a plan, and nothing about bans", function () {
    expect(res.body.account.id).to.be.a("string").and.not.be.empty;
    expect(res.body.account.plan).to.equal("free");
    expect(res.body.account).to.not.have.property("banned_at");
  });

  test("carries no password digest under any name", function () {
    expect(JSON.stringify(res.body)).to.not.match(/\$2[aby]\$/);
  });

  test("sets no cookie — nothing in this API can", function () {
    expect(res.getHeader("set-cookie")).to.be.undefined;
  });
}

docs {
  FR-001. Who the caller is, read from the row rather than from the token.

  ## Why this endpoint exists

  The dashboard needs an address for its header and a role to decide whether to
  draw the Admin item. Both were briefly carried to the browser in a readable
  `snip_account` cookie the Next.js BFF wrote at sign-in, and that was wrong
  twice over:

  - it put a person's email address in a cookie attached to requests that had no
    use for it, including — on a machine where the API and the short domain
    share a host — a visitor following a short link;
  - it froze the answer at the moment the session opened. An address corrected
    or a role granted afterwards stayed invisible until the session was closed
    and opened again, because nothing rewrote the cookie.

  A request answers both objections, so the cookie was deleted. Nothing in the
  browser now describes the session except the two httpOnly credentials
  themselves.

  ## `role` here is display, not authority

  Authorisation reads the `role` claim inside the signed access token, on every
  request. That claim is only rewritten at a refresh, so for up to fifteen
  minutes this endpoint and the token may disagree — a freshly promoted admin
  sees the Admin item and gets 403 behind it until the token catches up.

  That is safe only while nothing authorises on this value, which is why the
  serializer's field list is asserted in `spec/requests/api/v1/me_spec.rb` and
  why `AccountSerializer` says so in a comment. Do not add a permissions array
  here.

  ## Variations worth pasting in by hand

      (no Authorization header)          → 401 unauthenticated
      Authorization: Bearer nonsense     → 401 unauthenticated
      (a token older than fifteen minutes) → 401 token_expired

  The last one is the code the whole client design rests on: `token_expired`
  means "refresh and retry", and this is usually the first request a dashboard
  makes, so it is where the browser most often meets it.
}
